Advanced Motor Neurone Disease
Palliative & End of Life
Progressive generalized weakness, Severe bulbar palsy/dysphagia, Respiratory muscle failure

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-005
Reason for Visit: Acute respiratory distress and severe choking episode in a patient with progressive bulbar and respiratory weakness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient experienced a severe choking episode on their own secretions, leading to acute respiratory distress, loud stridor, and cyanosis. Urgent after-hours medical assessment was mandatory to clear the airway, manage secretions, and prevent impending type 2 respiratory failure or asphyxiation prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Spouse states, "He started choking on his saliva, turned blue, and now he is fighting for every single breath."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a known history of progressive, profound, and generalized flaccid and spastic muscle weakness. Recently, severe bulbar palsy has directly caused intractable dysphagia and relentless, pooling drooling. Severe dysarthria has rapidly progressed to complete, irreversible anarthria; the patient currently communicates only via eye movements and a spelling board. Today, the patient suffered a severe choking episode. There is unremitting respiratory muscle weakness leading to signs of terminal type 2 respiratory failure. The spouse notes widespread, visible muscle fasciculations particularly prominent in the limbs. The patient has exhibited pathological laughing and crying indicating a profound pseudobulbar affect. Notably, the patient has intact, completely preserved sensory function and fully preserved cognitive function. Severe, visually striking muscular atrophy is causing complete functional decline.
Functional Status: Totally dependent, wheelchair-bound, unable to clear own secretions.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk of fatal aspiration; spouse is competent but deeply traumatized by the acute choking event.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/85 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 28 bpm | SpO2: 89% on room air | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious, orthopneic, utilizing accessory muscles, drooling profusely.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam shows shallow tidal volumes, paradoxical abdominal breathing, and transmitted upper airway rhonchi. Neurological exam reveals profound generalized atrophy, brisk reflexes, widespread fasciculations, and an absent gag reflex. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by observing the progressive generalized weakness (atrophy, inability to move limbs), severe bulbar palsy/dysphagia (drooling, anarthria), and respiratory muscle failure (paradoxical breathing, hypoxia).
Environmental Observations: Non-invasive ventilation (BIPAP) machine is at the bedside. Portable suction machine is present and functional.
Medication Reconciliation: Currently taking riluzole and PRN anticholinergics. Doses of anticholinergics have been missed today.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Advanced Motor Neurone Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Secretion Retention & Respiratory Distress)
Differentials:

Foreign body airway obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely as the choking was explicitly on endogenous saliva, and partial airflow is clearly maintained.

Acute pulmonary embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of sudden pleuritic chest pain, and the obvious pooling of upper airway secretions explaining the distress.

Lower respiratory tract infection (Pneumonia) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, productive purulent cough, and focal crepitations on auscultation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, life-threatening crisis of secretion retention and impending respiratory muscle failure secondary to advanced Motor Neurone Disease. The objective presence of hypoxia (SpO2 89%), severe tachypnea, pooling secretions, and paradoxical breathing indicates an immediate threat to the airway and ventilation. The patient is unable to clear their own airway due to severe bulbar palsy. Delaying medical care until standard operating hours would inevitably lead to fatal asphyxiation or irreversible hypercapnic respiratory failure. Immediate after-hours intervention was critical to perform oral suctioning, administer subcutaneous anticholinergics to halt secretion production, and optimize non-invasive ventilation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed immediate deep oral suctioning to clear the airway. Administered stat subcutaneous glycopyrrolate to rapidly reduce salivary secretions. Assisted spouse in applying the patient's BIPAP machine.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you hear the breathing becoming wet or rattling (trigger), use the suction machine immediately before the secretions pool (behavior), which will keep the airway clear and prevent panic and choking (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the respiratory/palliative care team for review of secretion management and BIPAP settings.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure backup suction catheters and battery power for the BIPAP are readily available.
Follow-up: Multidisciplinary MND clinic review within 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops severe respiratory distress that is unresponsive to suctioning and BIPAP, or if the patient loses consciousness, the spouse must immediately administer the prescribed crisis subcutaneous midazolam and morphine, and call 000 if hospital transfer is aligned with the patient's Advanced Care Directive.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Treatment is focused on aggressive symptom management and maintaining airway patency.
Clinical Course: Bulbar and respiratory weakness will continue to progress. Management of secretions and breathlessness is the absolute priority.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Use the suction machine proactively. If severe choking occurs again and cannot be cleared, administer the emergency comfort medications as previously planned.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The medication given under the skin will dry up the saliva so there is less to choke on. The BIPAP machine will do the physical work of breathing for the weakened chest muscles.
Actionable Steps: Keep the head of the bed elevated at least 45 degrees at all times. Perform oral suctioning every 2 hours or PRN.

